(1852798, 91073, 47653, '<td valign="top">The saddle is at 11,700 feet, IIRC. Last time I was there, at this very spot, I had the worst AMS I''ve ever had, and saw the highest altitude <a href="//www.headwes.com/humphreyspeak/page4.html" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">helicopter evacuation</a> I''ve ever personally witnessed (okay, sample of three, but even so, it was a nailbiter.) Oh, and on the way down the remnants of hurrican Xaiver rolled through. <br/> <br/>Yeah, that sign brings back some memories.<br/> <br/>That being said, I didn''t find the mountain itself especially photogenic, so I think you were wise to focus on the sign and the clouds. The harsh mid-day sun at these high altitudes lends itself to excessive contrast, as seen in the edges of the sign, but overall you''ve done a wonderful job of composing to limit this effect.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-11-09 21:07:05.</i></td>', 'The saddle is at 11,700 feet, IIRC. Last time I was there, at this very spot, I had the worst AMS I''ve ever had, and saw the highest altitude helicopter evacuation I''ve ever personally witnessed (okay, sample of three, but even so, it was a nailbiter.) Oh, and on the way down the remnants of hurrican Xaiver rolled through.  Yeah, that sign brings back some memories. That being said, I didn''t find the mountain itself especially photogenic, so I think you were wise to focus on the sign and the clouds. The harsh mid-day sun at these high altitudes lends itself to excessive contrast, as seen in the edges of the sign, but overall you''ve done a wonderful job of composing to limit this effect.', '2005-11-09 21:01:17.000000', '2005-11-09 21:07:05.000000', 0),
